  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA pleasant November day is in store for the area today. 1AM water \nvapor imagery shows a positively tilted upper level ridge extending \nfrom northern California to the Northern Rockies. The only feature \nof note, although largely insignificant, is a weak vorticity maximum \nover western Montana. This feature will traverse over northwestern \nand northern Wyoming late morning through the early afternoon bring \nincreased high level clouds to the area and some breezy winds. \nBreezy winds are forecast to occur in the Upper Wind River Basin, \naround Cody and the Absarokas, across Natrona County, and northern \nJohnson County. Otherwise, high temperatures range from the upper \n40s to mid 50s for locations west of the Continental Divide and 50s \nto low 60s east of the Continental Divide. \n\nOn Sunday, flow aloft becomes more zonal and strengthens as the \naforementioned ridge folds over and flattens. Overall warmer \ntemperatures are expected due to a surface high shifting into the \nIntermountain West, 700mb temperatures in the 3C to 9C degree range, \nand breezy southwest winds ahead of a weak Sunday evening cold \nfront. These 700mb temperatures translate to 50s for areas west of \nthe Continental Divide and upper 50s to 60s for most locations east \nof the Continental Divide. Johnson County will be the warmest with \nhighs in the low 70s. There is a 90% chance that winds will exceed \n30 mph across the Absarokas, eastern Wind River Mountains, and from \nSouth Pass to Casper and into southeast Johnson County. Winds are \nforecast to be rather light elsewhere. Given the abnormally warm \ntemperatures, gusty winds, and relative humidity values around 20% \n(80% to 90% chance) across Johnson County, elevated fire weather \nconditions are expected Sunday afternoon.\n\nThe Sunday evening cold front will be inconsequential, with only \nslightly cooler temperatures forecast for Monday. There is high \nconfidence in fairly strong zonal flow (60 to 80kts) aloft on \nMonday. This means that breezy winds with gusts around 20 to 25 mph \nare expected in the same locations as Sunday in addition to across \nYellowstone National Park and near Dubois. There is consensus \namongst model guidance that a shortwave will trek across the area on \nTuesday. There looks to be just enough mid-level moisture for light \nprecipitation to occur across northwest Wyoming. However, chances of \nanything greater than 0.01\" of liquid tops out at 40%. Another ridge \nis forecast build across the western CONUS Wednesday and shift into \nthe Intermountain West for Thursday and Friday. This means that the \noverall dry conditions and much above normal temperatures is \nforecast to persist into mid-November.",
